each image is a revolution in astronomy  the james webb telescope has seen deeper  into the universe than ever before  revealed new details of the death of a  star like our sun  exposed the black hole at the heart of a  distant dusty galaxy seen water in the  atmosphere of a planet more than a  thousand light years from earth  and these are just its first five images  no wonder they were more than a little  excited at nasa its boss arriving to  scientists turn cheerleaders dressed up  as the telescope's 18 gold-plated  mirrors these are just the first  glimpses  imagine what we're going to learn over  the course of 20 years  uh think of uh  the answers that we're going to get to  questions that we don't even know what  the questions are  it is extraordinary  there was hype around these images but  the faces of the scientists and  engineers seeing them for the first time  don't lie if you're any doubt about how  significant these new images are i wish  you could be in this room because around  me are the scientists the  astrophysicists the cosmologists who  really understand how they've changed  our view of the universe not only can  they see things in incredible new detail  already just with these first five  images we've seen things we've never  seen before i think one of the great  things about this telescope is how  efficient it is we're able to get this  this kind of data in a fraction of the  time that we were before so i think a  lot of the records that we have in the  current day universe those are going to  be broken really soon  there was a sense of relief too the webb  telescope is the largest ever put in  space it took 20 years and 10 billion  dollars to design and build and it was  nearly cancelled many times  even after launch hundreds of things  could have gone wrong as it unfolded  itself a million miles from earth there  are many moments where i was really  really worried about the future  including at the beginning frankly even  five six years ago where we were  standing still burning money we could  not make progress and we needed to build  an amazing team there were many moments  like that but all of them are behind us  now and the  sky is open for us  within days we're expecting webb to give  us a new view of the planets in our  solar system within weeks see the very  first light that illuminated the  universe 13.5 billion years ago and it's  expected to work for another 20 years  so prepare to be amazed  tom clark sky news maryland
 

